BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Santa Paws - Pictures with Santa for Dogs and Cats
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261206T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261206T140000
UID:261126d63zdzEQX06HdymSFOtjYg
DESCRIPTION:Santa is at the distillery\, and he's taking pictures with dog
 s and cats. Bring yours to Barber Lee Spirits Distillery on Sunday\, Decem
 ber 6\, from 11 am to 2 pm. Cats may have opinions about this. Bring them 
 anyway.\n\nThe event is in partnership with Sage Compassion for Animals\, 
 a Bay Area nonprofit that helps families pay for veterinary care when a pe
 t is in a medical crisis. When a dog or cat needs emergency treatment and 
 the bill is more than a family can cover\, SageC4A steps in so the pet get
 s care and stays home with the people who love it. They also teach pet own
 ers how to prevent illness before it becomes an emergency.\n\nA portion of
  the day's proceeds goes to that work. SageC4A will be on site to answer q
 uestions\, with a donation jar and QR codes if you'd like to give more.\n\
 nBarber Lee makes spirits\, but there's also wine\, non-alcoholic drinks\,
  food\, chocolate\, and cheese. Kids are welcome. Stay a while.
LOCATION:Barber Lee Spirits
URL:https://www.barberleespirits.com/events/santa-paws
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:"Lessons and Carols" concert Dec. 6 in Healdsburg
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261206T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261206T180000
UID:261124dmcl4O49U0ilpz8XOoqQHQ
DESCRIPTION:The musicians of St. Paul's Episcopal Church present a traditi
 onal "Lessons and Carols" concert at 5 p.m. Sunday\, Dec. 6\, at the churc
 h in Healdsburg. The public is welcome to attend and sing traditional Chri
 stmas carols while hearing the St. Paul's choir accompanied by Music Direc
 tor Paul Blanchard playing the Bigelow pipe organ. There is no admission f
 ee.\n\nFor 146 years "Lessons and Carols"  has told the story of the fal
 l of humanity\, the promise of the Messiah and the birth of Jesus in nine 
 short Bible readings interspersed with the singing of Christmas carols\, h
 ymns and choir anthems. The service was first performed in 1880 in Cornwal
 l\, and it slowly spread throughout Great Britain. It was first performed 
 at King's College\, Cambridge\, in 1918. The service has been held every y
 ear since and is broadcast on BBC radio and television several times each 
 Christmas Eve and Christmas Day. The service was first performed in the Un
 ited States in 1916\; and while its language is regularly updated and mode
 rnized\, the backbone of the service remains virtually unchanged from its 
 origins. Every year King's College receives recordings from around the glo
 be\, from the West Indies to the Far East\, adding to its collection of pe
 ople who celebrate the Christmas season by singing "Lessons and Carols."\n
 \nSt. Paul's Episcopal Church is located at the corner of East and Matheso
 n Streets\, one block east of Healdsburg's downtown Plaza. Nearby parking 
 is free. Those who cannot attend the concert in person are welcome to view
  it by visiting the church's concert series website\, www.stpauls-healdsbu
 rg.org/sunday\\_organ\\_series.
LOCATION:St. Paul's Episcopal Church
URL:http://www.stpauls-healdsburg.org/sunday_organ_series
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Discovering Women Poets\, a Poetry Competition
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261206T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261206T235900
UID:2611244mC79FOzVU-CdUuR_AS67A
DESCRIPTION:The National League of American Pen Women \\(NLAPW\\) and the 
 Golden Gate-Marin Branch are launching a poetry competition for women over
  18 years of age who are not currently members of the NLAPW. This regional
  and national competition will be run at the Branch level in the semi-fina
 list phase. Local winners are automatically entered into the League’s Na
 tional competition.\n\nWinners at the Branch level will receive $35 and ha
 ve their entry published in the Branch newsletter. They will also be invit
 ed to present their poetry in person or by Zoom.\n\nBranch winners become 
 semi-finalists in the National competition\, which will award $100 to thre
 e winners. National winners will have their work and a short bio published
  in The Pen Woman\, a national magazine. All semi-finalists will be listed
  in the magazine.\n\nUp to three poems may be submitted for one entry fee 
 of $15. Each poem should be no longer than 40 lines. There are no restrict
 ions on topics or poem’s structure. Entries must be received by January 
 25\, 2027.\n\nDownload Entry Form at our website: https://www.goldengatema
 rinart.org/copy-of-contact
LOCATION:https://www.goldengatemarinart.org
URL:https://www.goldengatemarinart.org/copy-of-contact
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
